// fuzz_oracle.py (boundary literals, 11-01): a float literal that rounds to
// infinity. laniusc rejects it, as Rust does: LNC0070 "this finite literal
// overflows f64". The verified front end used to round it with ofDecimal to
// +inf and compile it (exit 1). Decided 11-01 (SEMANTIC_DECISIONS.md, "Float
// literals in the front end"): both reject it; frontend.lani's float_finite
// drops a literal whose bits at the picked type are infinite. The same holds for
// f32 (`1e39`, `3.4028236e38`), for a negated literal, and for 400 digits
// before the point. Literals that round down to the largest finite value
// (`1.7976931348623158e308`, `3.4028235e38`) and literals that underflow
// (`1e-400`) are still read, alike in both.
// Run: python3 -I formal/tools/fuzz_oracle.py WORKDIR --program THIS
module app::main;

fn main() -> i32 {
    let f: f64 = 1e400;
    let m: f64 = 1e308;
    if (f > m) {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}
